<div class="Page__contentArea"><div>
<div id="254be2d3"><section id="key-information" class="tDwjVFExbPlqwnaU86Wz"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF tNNRbfeiPsdi3j_gpC7k">Key information</h2><ul class="dE8scaebAaiVDo4BSvWD jQbeCSjNzeFEwKyP9FS0"><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-check-circle"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V">Future-oriented</h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark"><span>You gain the skills needed in an increasing number of industries.</span></div></div></li><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-check-circle"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V">Specialization</h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark"><span>Choose a concentration in interior architecture or visual communication design.</span></div></div></li><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-check-circle"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V"><span>Recognized academic environment</span></h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark"><span>Our lecturers have extensive experience in the industry you are training for.</span></div></div></li><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-error-circle"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V">Academic department</h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark"><a href="/en/faculties/faculty-of-health-sciences-and-technology/">School of Economics, Innovation, and Technology</a></div></div></li><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-user"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V">Admission requirements</h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark"><span>The program has specific admission requirements. You must also submit an entrance exam. Read more at the bottom of the page.</span></div></div></li><li class="iIoZpbf7rJjFaPayfYGd"><div class="i9mvzE2ZVt1U_mgl53yZ NBkadhPtSiaEbuC1H71t"><div class="_HPUHJNlNHRSzaUJfTVb"><span class="SvgIcon tA8I0a90PWzmxCh2AhM3"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-credit-card"></use></svg></span><h3 class="ecyElYvuOSSL_6pZBv9V">Tuition fees</h3></div><div class="cZ1dIE6oJgjjufj0oSwG RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark">65 600 kr per semester.&nbsp;<a href="/en/study-at-kristiania/price-overview/"><span>Additional information about prices can be found here.</span></a></div></div></li></ul><ul class="KdKtz5eB9ExUSDYJcdj8"><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Level"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-book"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">Master</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Start date"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-clock"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">Fall 2026</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Mode of study"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-activity"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">Full-time</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Points"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-award"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">120 Points</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="City"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-map-pin"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">Oslo</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Duration"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-calendar"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">2 years</span></div></li><li><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN lgn5jtFtWmCfY3jJKvE1 _YiOaSqqzZqBucsGKGGx U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5" title="Language"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-message-square"></use></svg></span><span class="usSRVYlqJnoDeG8iYItA">English</span></div></li></ul></div></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['254be2d3-json'] = {"id":"key-information","title":{"value":"Key information"},"facts":[{"title":{"value":"Future-oriented"},"icon":"check-circle","content":{"value":"<span>You gain the skills needed in an increasing number of industries.</span>"}},{"title":{"value":"Specialization"},"icon":"check-circle","content":{"value":"<span>Choose a concentration in interior architecture or visual communication design.</span>"}},{"title":{"value":"<span>Recognized academic environment</span>"},"icon":"check-circle","content":{"value":"<span>Our lecturers have extensive experience in the industry you are training for.</span>"}},{"title":{"value":"Academic department"},"icon":"error-circle","content":{"value":"<a href=\"/en/faculties/faculty-of-health-sciences-and-technology/\">School of Economics, Innovation, and Technology</a>"}},{"title":{"value":"Admission requirements"},"icon":"user","content":{"value":"<span>The program has specific admission requirements. You must also submit an entrance exam. Read more at the bottom of the page.</span>"}},{"title":{"value":"Tuition fees"},"icon":"credit-card","content":{"value":"65 600 kr per semester.&nbsp;<a href=\"/en/study-at-kristiania/price-overview/\"><span>Additional information about prices can be found here.</span></a>"}}],"tags":[{"title":"Level","icon":"book","value":"Master"},{"title":"Start date","icon":"clock","value":"Fall 2026"},{"title":"Mode of study","icon":"activity","value":"Full-time"},{"title":"Points","icon":"award","value":"120 Points"},{"title":"City","icon":"map-pin","value":"Oslo"},{"title":"Duration","icon":"calendar","value":"2 years"},{"title":"Language","icon":"message-square","value":"English"}]};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="db6927e5"><section id="learning-outcomes" class="cwjtYbGmpj2Fc0XguMNM"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF aZkJAbcjSQdYr77t8LV7">Learning outcomes</h2><div class="zOMHtRvuigbQpkhq8l0i"><div class="FHwe9agruJdTg4dYFHN9"><div class="FktOVJkMpTVphR1rGeNp RichtextContent RichtextContent--light"><span>In a world filled with &nbsp;complex challenges, design can contribute to positive societal change. Today&rsquo;s designers work increasingly across disciplines. This requires broad knowledge of society and business, as well as a good understanding of one&rsquo;s own professional practice.</span>The goal of this master's degree is to educate candidates who can critically reflect on design practices and who can contribute to defining what design can and should be in the society of the future.</p>
<p>In the Master's in Design, you can choose between two specializations:</p>
<ul class="Richtext__ul">
<li>Interior Architecture</li>
<li>Visual Communication Design"</li>
</ul></div><button title="More about the education" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_45601" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT f_wbJQigmJWOUtJRHvmX"><span class="visuallyHidden">More about the education</span><span class="SvgIcon Kw06kOpEjKtbccDsYKVG"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-plus-circle"></use></svg></span></button><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_45601" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_45602"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_45602" class="visuallyHidden">More about the education</span><h3 class="vGAYXQ8qVh3muyCADVi0">More about the education</h3><div class="RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark">Kristiania's Master's in Design is suitable for students who have high academic ambitions and who are concerned with the role of design in society&mdash;whether from a close and everyday perspective or a global and long-term perspective. Furthermore, the program is suitable for those who have an open, investigative, and experimental approach to their creative practice. With small classes, close follow-up, and access to excellent facilities, everything is in place for a rich and exciting study experience.</p>
<p>The teaching is adapted to the content of the various subjects, but during the study period, you will encounter the following teaching methods:</p>
<ul class="Richtext__ul">
<li>Lectures/presentation and instruction</li>
<li>Guidance and formative assessment</li>
<li>Case, group, and/or project work</li>
<li>Workshops and seminars</li>
<li>Study groups and assignment work</li>
<li>Independent practice/lab work/practical work individually or in groups</li>
<li>Independent academic work with curriculum and other source material</li>
<li>Other student activities, including presentations, plenary discussions, and communication</li>
<li>Business contact</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Learning by doing</strong></p>
<ul class="Richtext__ul">
<li>As a Kristiania student, your daily life will be filled with creative exploration, reflection, and practical work. The program combines theory and practice and is based on a critical and investigative approach to the field.</li>
<li>In the design field, learning often occurs through "thinking with your hands". Therefore, you will have the opportunity to create, test, and reflect alongside fellow students and lecturers in workshops, case assignments, project work, and much more.</li>
<li>The dynamic between creating and reflecting, asking questions and commenting, can lead to new insights and invites students to challenge established truths.</li>
<li>You will receive close guidance from professionals and have the opportunity to work on real-world issues in collaboration with actors from the design industry.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How we work</strong></p>
<ul class="Richtext__ul">
<li>Kristiania has Norway's largest creative and artistic environment at the university and college level. You will find our students in all creative industries both domestically and internationally.</li>
<li><span>Our lecturers have extensive industry experience and are active researchers in their fields,</span><span>&nbsp;</span>and we likely have some of the country's finest facilities for those who want to pursue a creative education. We know what awaits you in the workforce and will challenge you every day so that you succeed.</li>
<li>We have a close relationship with our students. We strive for students to have high trust in us and each other, making it easier to challenge themselves and one another. There should be room for a high degree of experimentation, and we encourage students to explore new ways of being creative. We believe in practical work, often interdisciplinary. Therefore, our facilities will be the same as those you will encounter when you enter the workforce.</li>
</ul></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="qB4kMuPluv5Id4WqPmfn"></div><div class="uMSWbISaLHTvPMbXhnYp"><div class="slJnKPGAAIKofsQwQBjL vPKYJ9xnnqorRitmkm3i R1wh_51Fi_Crd9RnsQsw"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, (min-width: 768px) 552px" srcSet="/contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg?width=375 375w, /contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg?width=552 552w, /contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg?width=768 768w, /contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg?width=1536 1536w"/><img src="/contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg?width=552" alt="En person blar i tekstilprøver" style="object-position:70% 28.000000000000004%" loading="lazy"/></picture></div></div></div></div></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['db6927e5-json'] = {"id":"learning-outcomes","title":{"value":"Learning outcomes"},"content":{"value":"<span>In a world filled with &nbsp;complex challenges, design can contribute to positive societal change. Today&rsquo;s designers work increasingly across disciplines. This requires broad knowledge of society and business, as well as a good understanding of one&rsquo;s own professional practice.</span>The goal of this master's degree is to educate candidates who can critically reflect on design practices and who can contribute to defining what design can and should be in the society of the future.</p>\n<p>In the Master's in Design, you can choose between two specializations:</p>\n<ul class=\"Richtext__ul\">\n<li>Interior Architecture</li>\n<li>Visual Communication Design\"</li>\n</ul>"},"overlayTitle":"More about the education","overlayContent":{"value":"Kristiania's Master's in Design is suitable for students who have high academic ambitions and who are concerned with the role of design in society&mdash;whether from a close and everyday perspective or a global and long-term perspective. Furthermore, the program is suitable for those who have an open, investigative, and experimental approach to their creative practice. With small classes, close follow-up, and access to excellent facilities, everything is in place for a rich and exciting study experience.</p>\n<p>The teaching is adapted to the content of the various subjects, but during the study period, you will encounter the following teaching methods:</p>\n<ul class=\"Richtext__ul\">\n<li>Lectures/presentation and instruction</li>\n<li>Guidance and formative assessment</li>\n<li>Case, group, and/or project work</li>\n<li>Workshops and seminars</li>\n<li>Study groups and assignment work</li>\n<li>Independent practice/lab work/practical work individually or in groups</li>\n<li>Independent academic work with curriculum and other source material</li>\n<li>Other student activities, including presentations, plenary discussions, and communication</li>\n<li>Business contact</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Learning by doing</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"Richtext__ul\">\n<li>As a Kristiania student, your daily life will be filled with creative exploration, reflection, and practical work. The program combines theory and practice and is based on a critical and investigative approach to the field.</li>\n<li>In the design field, learning often occurs through \"thinking with your hands\". Therefore, you will have the opportunity to create, test, and reflect alongside fellow students and lecturers in workshops, case assignments, project work, and much more.</li>\n<li>The dynamic between creating and reflecting, asking questions and commenting, can lead to new insights and invites students to challenge established truths.</li>\n<li>You will receive close guidance from professionals and have the opportunity to work on real-world issues in collaboration with actors from the design industry.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How we work</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"Richtext__ul\">\n<li>Kristiania has Norway's largest creative and artistic environment at the university and college level. You will find our students in all creative industries both domestically and internationally.</li>\n<li><span>Our lecturers have extensive industry experience and are active researchers in their fields,</span><span>&nbsp;</span>and we likely have some of the country's finest facilities for those who want to pursue a creative education. We know what awaits you in the workforce and will challenge you every day so that you succeed.</li>\n<li>We have a close relationship with our students. We strive for students to have high trust in us and each other, making it easier to challenge themselves and one another. There should be room for a high degree of experimentation, and we encourage students to explore new ways of being creative. We believe in practical work, often interdisciplinary. Therefore, our facilities will be the same as those you will encounter when you enter the workforce.</li>\n</ul>"},"gobiStoryId":null,"imageFallback":{"author":null,"caption":null,"url":"/contentassets/f75f82107f6a40948c3934d7208b06dc/due_2158-3-1.jpg","alt":"En person blar i tekstilprøver","focalPoint":{"x":0.7,"y":0.28},"lazyLoading":true},"translations":{"openOverlay":"More about the education","closeOverlay":"Close overlay","openPlayer":"Open player","failedToLoadVideo":"Sorry, for some reason we could not load a video"}};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="c5cac72d"><section id="study-model" class="eXDLkCBTey4RjrTQSVwU"><div class="coBlOy78vCNQoCaBJMWq"><div class="fIm4MtcrG6MCKKd8iidQ"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF A9Jn0XOSrtngUTCr8gcL">Study model</h2><div class="t78ozlVAuwJUrQItlgiY"><span class="IKcqvZpEOT2PNX0vgEn0">This master&#x27;s degree lasts two academic years, and each academic year is divided into two semesters. Here you can see an overview of compulsory subjects and what opportunities you have for practice, exchange and specialization. We reserve the right to make changes.</span></div></div></div><div class="DwbrqOYPLpXMxa_AUi5a"><div class="c4EHzShG0bxjcWsv1iba _jn80nJpcKPRabCSPcI8 o7i_4ji7xZ2pdim0DGGS r6PBO9Z23nGvfMtmlwMK dT6kOO1SWAAgm3_8NHMn"><div class="w6rdwzyeKdZC4QEQQdUG"><div class="FQnvPH3eSTnAU6bvrkhn"><div class="IbD8iYA8j6gRUkFt19Qt nUUm_USrpFGrNosOvcCN gXLqKND3fX0_VG9NQewb"><div role="tablist" class="_NQp5NLSF5eDFXAqjrMf" aria-label="Select year"><button id="tab_1" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tabPanel_1" type="button" class="wSwA7V6mKnVhr5xFafzq Sqayds_IfbVRGAlPcZz1"><span class="bAoReRDLMgoAniQxqdep">First year</span></button><button id="tab_2" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabPanel_2" type="button" class="wSwA7V6mKnVhr5xFafzq"><span class="bAoReRDLMgoAniQxqdep">Second year</span></button></div></div><span class="FaaFQQsQ1uH8384yNffh">120 total credits</span></div><div id="tabPanel_1" class="xD2nuHiyJc0KNBTM5TWg" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab_1"><div class="XtdQZg9bKtEzGXT39oxb"><div style="will-change:height;overflow:visible;height:auto" class="wpyTqNTlEGsGZ_xfFehk L7NlqGAknErj1_Ksfbxg"><div class="XjUXAnCLxrJU_S3Faibn km5O6Vr04dX9qXqlrGMV"><div id="comboSelect_id46527" class="bbDHU4_JdA0hl9MD1Lrq">Specialization:</div><fieldset aria-describedby="comboSelect_id46527"><div class="drG0qWE9kXwq4x52jnrJ"><div class="Sp19XW4DI_sJH0_4zvTF"><div class="PHAoRS_eP5FouDCPNKdY GhAWyApR_JjGprSMJ2rM yje2hGysH1zQf_D0GsA7 echijgywrVRRk64pe6Cg"><input type="radio" id="FormComponents__radio__46528" class="YfHgbQjwjs4O8VWhll1c" name="specialization-selector" checked="" value="Interiørarkitektur"/><label for="FormComponents__radio__46528" class="XTEgAUAWtLfl5XRg_bT7"><span class="yUjgOv16CeoVvFj3U4nh">Interiørarkitektur</span></label></div><div class="PHAoRS_eP5FouDCPNKdY GhAWyApR_JjGprSMJ2rM yje2hGysH1zQf_D0GsA7 echijgywrVRRk64pe6Cg"><input type="radio" id="FormComponents__radio__46529" class="YfHgbQjwjs4O8VWhll1c" name="specialization-selector" value="Interiørarkitektur"/><label for="FormComponents__radio__46529" class="XTEgAUAWtLfl5XRg_bT7"><span class="yUjgOv16CeoVvFj3U4nh">Visuell kommunikasjonsdesign</span></label></div></div></div></fieldset></div></div><div style="will-change:height;overflow:hidden;visibility:hidden;height:0" class="wpyTqNTlEGsGZ_xfFehk BK3qq6WSy_IM14HivUP2"><div class="XjUXAnCLxrJU_S3Faibn km5O6Vr04dX9qXqlrGMV Wc0G2UOdAM4L2tA07AtA"><div id="comboSelect_id46530" class="bbDHU4_JdA0hl9MD1Lrq">Options:</div><fieldset aria-describedby="comboSelect_id46530"><div class="drG0qWE9kXwq4x52jnrJ"><div class="Sp19XW4DI_sJH0_4zvTF"><div class="PHAoRS_eP5FouDCPNKdY GhAWyApR_JjGprSMJ2rM yje2hGysH1zQf_D0GsA7 echijgywrVRRk64pe6Cg"><input type="radio" id="FormComponents__radio__46531" class="YfHgbQjwjs4O8VWhll1c" name="study-option-selector" checked="" value="Default path"/><label for="FormComponents__radio__46531" class="XTEgAUAWtLfl5XRg_bT7"><span class="yUjgOv16CeoVvFj3U4nh">Default path</span></label></div><div class="PHAoRS_eP5FouDCPNKdY GhAWyApR_JjGprSMJ2rM yje2hGysH1zQf_D0GsA7 echijgywrVRRk64pe6Cg"><input type="radio" id="FormComponents__radio__46532" class="YfHgbQjwjs4O8VWhll1c" name="study-option-selector" value="Default path"/><label for="FormComponents__radio__46532" class="XTEgAUAWtLfl5XRg_bT7"><span class="yUjgOv16CeoVvFj3U4nh">Exchange</span></label></div></div></div></fieldset></div></div><span class="NPFkgrIiXfIBoY50mCiN">60 credits</span><div class="osNFPrEfj_WDLlms79Vw"><div class="gIhCJK5vlYW4N6yeTlUe C70d_1unGsLcavciTBWP g8GJRoyN3YYrck7buLCF" style="grid-template-columns:auto repeat(5, minmax(min-content, 1fr))"><div class="PVTUTdX7fee_uJTuRkiD ihFnGka9xDug5NPWFh0C"></div><div class="PH6z9gi7b9kHBB9jOhSt nPbPqGrz5Ue0OKa68YnY z7vhCTYrC3rO2VoUPlYT">1. semester</div><div class="PH6z9gi7b9kHBB9jOhSt nPbPqGrz5Ue0OKa68YnY _MLObcIsnBTnfP4F5AQg">2. semester</div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:5 / span 1;grid-row:1"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46533" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">Design and Society</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">DSA4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">7.5<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46533" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46534"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46534" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">Design and Society</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>The course introduces the study program’s disciplinary core through exploration of the relationship between society, the design field, and the designer. The students are introduced to a series of concepts in contemporary design discourse. Through discussions and practical work, they reflect on disciplinary/professional and ethical problems that today’s designers are faced with.</p><p>The course has an overarching perspective on design, and it is up to the students themselves to contextualise the knowledge within their own specialisations.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:6 / span 1;grid-row:1"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46535" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">Abstraction</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">IAB4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN ftxYN6Cf0R37irTe8gq3 jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA">Specialization</div><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">7.5<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46535" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46536"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46536" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">Abstraction</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>The course has an overall focus on abstraction as a method in creative work processes. The student must be able to analyse, identify, and evaluate the potential and possibilities of an idea. In the process, the student must use their own senses and experiences. The course has a clear focus on aesthetics, abstraction, senses, and phenomenology. In addition, emphasis is placed on reflection on one's own and others' practice.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:4 / span 1;grid-row:1"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46537" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">Design Research Methods</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">DFM4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">7.5<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46537" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46538"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46538" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">Design Research Methods</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>Design is a broad discipline with a great variety of research methods. Which methods are relevant for the individual project, the individual field, and the individual designer's interests, therefore span user-centred, art-oriented, innovative, and exploratory ones. The course presents a selection of methods for design research. It encourages reflection on the epistemological basis of these methods. The course presents methods for generating and developing design outcomes and research. It examines how a methodical approach to design activities can inform exploratory design practice and research in the arts. By presenting examples of how methods can be combined, modified, or adapted to specific issues and projects for students, the course facilitates discussion about what method in design is and can be. Throughout the course, students are encouraged to reflect on how to apply research methods in their own master's project and include these reflections in their own project outline.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:5 / span 2;grid-row:3"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46539" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">The Future</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">IAF4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN ftxYN6Cf0R37irTe8gq3 jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA">Specialization</div><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">15<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46539" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46540"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46540" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">The Future</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>The module has a focus on aesthetics, sustainability, and conceptual architecture with the intention that this will help the student to actively contribute to the world of tomorrow.</p><p>The students will gain an increased understanding of the relationship between the present and the future, together with an understanding of the role that design has in a world that is in dynamic change.</p><p>The module is divided into two clear parts: The first part is characterized by a period of deep insight, where the student is challenged to go through a period of self-reflection with the intention that this can lead to systemic changes on a personal level. The intention is that these changes will contribute to creating an architecture for the future.</p><p>The second phase of the project will be of a practical nature and will be focused on developing and presenting a design solution that builds on the insights gained in the first phase.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:2 / span 2;grid-row:1 / span 3"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46541" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">Master’s Project in Design 1</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">MDE4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">15<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46541" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46542"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46542" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">Master’s Project in Design 1</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>The master's project is each individual student's main project. It can consist of a single project or be composed of several smaller projects bound together by an overarching issue and written reflection. All or parts of the master's project can be individual, a collaboration between several students, or a collaboration with external actors.</p><p>The Master's project requires both a methodical approach to the topic to be explored, a high level of practical, technical, and craftsmanship skills, as well as advanced theoretical knowledge and the ability to link this to the practical part of the project.</p><p>Through the work on the master's project, the students explore, among other things, practical, theoretical, and ethical challenges linked to working with publicly accessible design work and design as a social and cultural phenomenon. This work provides knowledge and experience in solving complex design problems, and the ability to critically reflect on one's own process and solution is central. The work can take different forms and have different end results, but must be adapted to the student's specialisation.</p><p>In order to make the student aware of the professional reflection inherent in design work, and so that others can take part in and benefit from it, the project must also contain a written reflection. In this, contextual assumptions must be explained and discussed, and linked to the practical project. Through the written reflection, the student's learning must be articulated and made transferable to others.</p><p>The master's project should add new knowledge to the subject field in question. "Knowledge" is here a broad term, which can include new visual expressions, processes and methods, strategies, technical and artisanal solutions, theoretical connections, contexts, etc.</p><p>Upon submission, the master's project must be published or otherwise presented publicly, in a dissemination format approved by the supervisor.</p><p>The master's project is divided into two subjects, one in each study year. The subject Master's project in design, part 1 (in the first and second semesters), emphasizes the development of the project from the idea stage, via a project sketch that frames it and gives it direction, to the started project. In the Master's project in design, part 2 (in the third and fourth semesters), the student builds on this work and further develops and completes the master's project. Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 amount to a total of 52.5 credits.</p><p>Introduction to Master's project in design part 1</p><p>(NB! Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 must be seen in context, as the master's project covers both subjects. What is written in this course description is therefore also relevant for the subject Master's project in design part 2.)</p><p>In the subject Master's project in design part 1, students must develop a project sketch for the master's project as a whole, which is delivered at the end of the first semester. The project outline must present, i.a., the project's theme and problem, theoretical grounding, methods, as well as any practical, technical, local, and collaborative prerequisites for the project. The course ends after the second semester with a presentation of the project's status and a progress plan. The students receive feedback that lays the foundation for the work in the final subject (Master's project in design part 2).</p><p>It is expected that the students apply the knowledge and skills they have acquired in the other subjects in the first and second semesters.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="Ka1VIQ2BSAPjauIEHrxP" style="grid-column:4 / span 1;grid-row:3"><div role="none" class="jkzRoAa60jRsn7WFC38b W_ipeNxzDR7tVIgpSCZn" tabindex="-1"><h3 class="YG1nlAZdhHLMRy9GYzR4"><button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="overlay_46543" aria-haspopup="dialog" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT H8BBXS4M_AkAanZLMEqS"><div class="Lv5sVd5u45Hei4dM91D6"><span class="cLkCE07zhLnMWRjri1dT">The Designer’s Position</span><span class="scgJDiGbZP69e6JD2e1j">DPO4100</span></div></button></h3><div class="k1UzcGuDdqoVJEx_3umq"><div class="QfpSu3R9osOn5mtmUmRN AK7QRdIj4UViy_jglQrq jno52DfczcFYoXwAJOAu U9uDXh99G9DWi_Yh96aO EPjsXy4Ah89AtUfpopbN Iga6BKVCOIbIKJLg1wb5 RNsXkIAukZjr8f4x_HXA iFv6rrcS3ouLeLPVmLv7">7.5<!-- --> <!-- -->credits</div></div></div><div style="display:none"><div class="QhFNlQLYGSl_aaOQtwwl" style="display:none" id="overlay_46543" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="overlayLabel_46544"><div class="lAKZZQugItoUqTlu8DRV"></div><div tabindex="-1"><div class="po18YUo5kkFOZqsiTnKR"><div class="eOdUoHIpgD7QwsaR1kGn"><button title="Close overlay" type="button" class="NtmtjnwY8FdZxOUBkRwT sZg2Vy20YIsqTNv9TcIX"><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-circle-close"></use></svg></span><span class="visuallyHidden">Close overlay</span></button></div><div class="fyYha0cYX3WzsCpMkhUB"><span id="overlayLabel_46544" class="visuallyHidden">Open subject overlay</span><div><h3 class="E3lyE2pUr4JODpEYbHMe">The Designer’s Position</h3><div class="vyo8UTe2MiSR4m0iLBYR">
<p>All design carries within it an attitude that arises from the value system in which the designer has grown up and been educated. Often, this attitude is invisible to us because we haven’t learned to recognize it. From this perspective, the designer—whether consciously or unconsciously—is an active co-creator of our society and culture.</p><p>The course is about becoming aware of these values and attitudes as a basis for defining one’s own position as a designer, and thereby becoming an informed co-creator. Critical examination and an external perspective of the field are used to help reveal implicit and unconscious attitudes and values. Concepts such as identity, class, diversity, power, intersectionality, and norm criticism are explored within the context of design, and the students are challenged to relate them to their own design practices.</p><p>The course incorporates knowledge from the course <em>Design and Society</em> from the first semester into the process of defining one’s own position. This work will also lay a significant foundation for the master’s project.</p>
</div><div class="iCNqjCRL4lMHUFqVaQaB"><button disabled="" type="button" class="yEK9biWpMxeKit4W3SEn HIBx2TMQpGodNFRbYUAk Pf5HgbFCxXwoDDfyfLsp cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE yWrwquZkc56N0bYjqb7s"><span>Read more</span><span class="SvgIcon"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-arrow-right"></use></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="hUrOK4f2t5d89i_al5eu _jBcyRZzOe6QMtbojFs5"></div></div></div></div></div><div class="_qtIUhem1sc1EKjP4UhQ"><div id="tabPanel_2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab_2"><ul><li><span>FOS5100</span><span>Dissemination Strategies</span><div>
<p>This course teaches outreach strategies and methodology for creating visibility in digital networks through community building and crowdsourcing. It covers how the active use of digital networks can give access to resources that can strengthen the artist’s career and entrepreneurship, productions, IP (Intellectual Property), or co-creation as part of artistic practice.      </p><p>This methodology of community interaction and co-creation is also used to create dissemination strategies that include traditional media and physical events. These strategies combine all these elements in communication strategies focusing on the artist’s profile, the artwork or research’s core messaging, and the analyzed relevance for the intended community.  </p><p>One of the outcomes of the course is an outreach plan for the master project, including a strategy for the public presentation of the work and communication of the reflection and results to peers and the general public, tailored for individual projects. </p>
</div></li><li><span>MDE5100</span><span>Master’s Project in Design 2</span><div>
<p>NB! Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 must be seen in context, as the master's project covers both courses. What is written in this course description is therefore also relevant for the course <em>Master's project in design part 1.</em></p><p>In the course <em>Master's project in design part 2</em>, the student further develops their design project, based on the work completed in the subject <em>Master's project in design part 1</em>, as well as knowledge and skills acquired in other courses. It is expected that the student is in regular dialogue with the main supervisor to deliver drafts and discuss the work and progression. The course ends with the submission of the master's project.</p>
</div></li><li><span>UTV999-MA-30</span><span>Utveksling</span><div></div></li><li><span>VAL999MA15</span><span>Valgemne</span><div>
<p lang="nb-NO" style="text-indent:0pt;margin-left:0pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;margin-right:0pt;font-size:11pt;">Du kan lese mer om dine valgmuligheter i dette semesteret</p>
<p lang="nb-NO" style="text-indent:0pt;margin-left:0pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;margin-right:0pt;font-size:11pt;color:#0000FF;text-decoration:underline ;"><a href="https://kristiania.no/for-studenter/valgemner-og-praksis-4/" style="font-size:11pt;color:#0000FF;text-decoration:underline ;">https://kristiania.no/for-studenter/valgemner-og-praksis-4/</a></p>
</div></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div><div class="lv4eNMiZgLixMXhAvacD"><a href="/en/syllabus/faculty-of-society-arts-and-media/second-cycle-degree/mde/master-in-design/" class="XezMfDvRba8HKhHr79wm UssovIOXlEuxxbOHY4c4 AMdryBi_G8WFVbcuUzSv LVwfmt8Qy2xfZW7GrHx_ I0dQ9KoNo8oj51pfedVE cJMgeHEGyMPOPNoeRPfY SwVWunM8J0KL5d8Ov54G" data-owned=""><div class="DdPYOTimBd1c8zDeoFgi">See program description</div></a></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['c5cac72d-json'] = {"heading":{"value":"Study model"},"getSubjectInfoUrl":"/api/study-pages/95114/subjects-info-url","overlayContentMessages":{"outdated":{"heading":"Subjected to change.","message":null,"variant":"info"},"fallback":{"heading":"Course description coming soon","message":{"value":"At Kristiania, we develop our studies in collaboration with working life. That's why we update the subjects regularly, so that you get the skills that employers are looking for. Soon you can read everything about this subject here, until then you will find more information in the study programme description."},"variant":"info"}},"subjects":[{"code":"DFM4100","name":"Design Research Methods","description":"\n<p>Design is a broad discipline with a great variety of research methods. Which methods are relevant for the individual project, the individual field, and the individual designer's interests, therefore span user-centred, art-oriented, innovative, and exploratory ones. The course presents a selection of methods for design research. It encourages reflection on the epistemological basis of these methods. The course presents methods for generating and developing design outcomes and research. It examines how a methodical approach to design activities can inform exploratory design practice and research in the arts. By presenting examples of how methods can be combined, modified, or adapted to specific issues and projects for students, the course facilitates discussion about what method in design is and can be. Throughout the course, students are encouraged to reflect on how to apply research methods in their own master's project and include these reflections in their own project outline.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":2,"points":7.5,"year":1,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"DPO4100","name":"The Designer’s Position","description":"\n<p>All design carries within it an attitude that arises from the value system in which the designer has grown up and been educated. Often, this attitude is invisible to us because we haven’t learned to recognize it. From this perspective, the designer—whether consciously or unconsciously—is an active co-creator of our society and culture.</p><p>The course is about becoming aware of these values and attitudes as a basis for defining one’s own position as a designer, and thereby becoming an informed co-creator. Critical examination and an external perspective of the field are used to help reveal implicit and unconscious attitudes and values. Concepts such as identity, class, diversity, power, intersectionality, and norm criticism are explored within the context of design, and the students are challenged to relate them to their own design practices.</p><p>The course incorporates knowledge from the course <em>Design and Society</em> from the first semester into the process of defining one’s own position. This work will also lay a significant foundation for the master’s project.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":4,"points":7.5,"year":1,"semester":"second","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"DSA4100","name":"Design and Society","description":"\n<p>The course introduces the study program’s disciplinary core through exploration of the relationship between society, the design field, and the designer. The students are introduced to a series of concepts in contemporary design discourse. Through discussions and practical work, they reflect on disciplinary/professional and ethical problems that today’s designers are faced with.</p><p>The course has an overarching perspective on design, and it is up to the students themselves to contextualise the knowledge within their own specialisations.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":1,"points":7.5,"year":1,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"FOS5100","name":"Dissemination Strategies","description":"\n<p>This course teaches outreach strategies and methodology for creating visibility in digital networks through community building and crowdsourcing. It covers how the active use of digital networks can give access to resources that can strengthen the artist’s career and entrepreneurship, productions, IP (Intellectual Property), or co-creation as part of artistic practice.      </p><p>This methodology of community interaction and co-creation is also used to create dissemination strategies that include traditional media and physical events. These strategies combine all these elements in communication strategies focusing on the artist’s profile, the artwork or research’s core messaging, and the analyzed relevance for the intended community.  </p><p>One of the outcomes of the course is an outreach plan for the master project, including a strategy for the public presentation of the work and communication of the reflection and results to peers and the general public, tailored for individual projects. </p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":true,"order":7,"points":7.5,"year":2,"semester":"second","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"MDE4100","name":"Master’s Project in Design 1","description":"\n<p>The master's project is each individual student's main project. It can consist of a single project or be composed of several smaller projects bound together by an overarching issue and written reflection. All or parts of the master's project can be individual, a collaboration between several students, or a collaboration with external actors.</p><p>The Master's project requires both a methodical approach to the topic to be explored, a high level of practical, technical, and craftsmanship skills, as well as advanced theoretical knowledge and the ability to link this to the practical part of the project.</p><p>Through the work on the master's project, the students explore, among other things, practical, theoretical, and ethical challenges linked to working with publicly accessible design work and design as a social and cultural phenomenon. This work provides knowledge and experience in solving complex design problems, and the ability to critically reflect on one's own process and solution is central. The work can take different forms and have different end results, but must be adapted to the student's specialisation.</p><p>In order to make the student aware of the professional reflection inherent in design work, and so that others can take part in and benefit from it, the project must also contain a written reflection. In this, contextual assumptions must be explained and discussed, and linked to the practical project. Through the written reflection, the student's learning must be articulated and made transferable to others.</p><p>The master's project should add new knowledge to the subject field in question. \"Knowledge\" is here a broad term, which can include new visual expressions, processes and methods, strategies, technical and artisanal solutions, theoretical connections, contexts, etc.</p><p>Upon submission, the master's project must be published or otherwise presented publicly, in a dissemination format approved by the supervisor.</p><p>The master's project is divided into two subjects, one in each study year. The subject Master's project in design, part 1 (in the first and second semesters), emphasizes the development of the project from the idea stage, via a project sketch that frames it and gives it direction, to the started project. In the Master's project in design, part 2 (in the third and fourth semesters), the student builds on this work and further develops and completes the master's project. Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 amount to a total of 52.5 credits.</p><p>Introduction to Master's project in design part 1</p><p>(NB! Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 must be seen in context, as the master's project covers both subjects. What is written in this course description is therefore also relevant for the subject Master's project in design part 2.)</p><p>In the subject Master's project in design part 1, students must develop a project sketch for the master's project as a whole, which is delivered at the end of the first semester. The project outline must present, i.a., the project's theme and problem, theoretical grounding, methods, as well as any practical, technical, local, and collaborative prerequisites for the project. The course ends after the second semester with a presentation of the project's status and a progress plan. The students receive feedback that lays the foundation for the work in the final subject (Master's project in design part 2).</p><p>It is expected that the students apply the knowledge and skills they have acquired in the other subjects in the first and second semesters.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":3,"points":15,"year":1,"semester":"both","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"MDE5100","name":"Master’s Project in Design 2","description":"\n<p>NB! Master's project in design parts 1 and 2 must be seen in context, as the master's project covers both courses. What is written in this course description is therefore also relevant for the course <em>Master's project in design part 1.</em></p><p>In the course <em>Master's project in design part 2</em>, the student further develops their design project, based on the work completed in the subject <em>Master's project in design part 1</em>, as well as knowledge and skills acquired in other courses. It is expected that the student is in regular dialogue with the main supervisor to deliver drafts and discuss the work and progression. The course ends with the submission of the master's project.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":5,"points":37.5,"year":2,"semester":"both","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":null,"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"IAB4100","name":"Abstraction","description":"\n<p>The course has an overall focus on abstraction as a method in creative work processes. The student must be able to analyse, identify, and evaluate the potential and possibilities of an idea. In the process, the student must use their own senses and experiences. The course has a clear focus on aesthetics, abstraction, senses, and phenomenology. In addition, emphasis is placed on reflection on one's own and others' practice.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":1,"points":7.5,"year":1,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":"Interiørarkitektur","studyOption":null,"category":"specialization"},{"code":"IAF4100","name":"The Future","description":"\n<p>The module has a focus on aesthetics, sustainability, and conceptual architecture with the intention that this will help the student to actively contribute to the world of tomorrow.</p><p>The students will gain an increased understanding of the relationship between the present and the future, together with an understanding of the role that design has in a world that is in dynamic change.</p><p>The module is divided into two clear parts: The first part is characterized by a period of deep insight, where the student is challenged to go through a period of self-reflection with the intention that this can lead to systemic changes on a personal level. The intention is that these changes will contribute to creating an architecture for the future.</p><p>The second phase of the project will be of a practical nature and will be focused on developing and presenting a design solution that builds on the insights gained in the first phase.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":2,"points":15,"year":1,"semester":"second","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":"Interiørarkitektur","studyOption":null,"category":"specialization"},{"code":"VFS4100","name":"Futures and Scenarios","description":"\n<p>The world is changing rapidly, both socially, politically, environmentally, and technologically. In this course, students look towards possible, likely, and preferred futures for individuals, groups, and societies based on current developments.</p><p>Students learn different tools within scenario planning and future analysis to construct, assess, and communicate different future scenarios through interdisciplinary collaboration. The future scenarios are based on in-depth primary and secondary research of a chosen social complex and communicated to a specific audience, such as politicians, business managers, or city/country residents.</p><p>Students explore critical, speculative, and discursive design projects to activate thought experiments, spark discussions, and promote new patterns of action in a developed future scenario. By exploring societal problems, visual communication design can illuminate future development.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":2,"points":15,"year":1,"semester":"second","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":"Visuell kommunikasjonsdesign","studyOption":null,"category":"specialization"},{"code":"VHL4100","name":"High-tech/Low-tech","description":"\n<p>Visual communication is produced and reproduced using technology. Technologies can be simple or advanced, old or new. The module develops the student's ability to use different technologies in creative ways in the development of relevant visual, tactile, and/or material expressions, from the artisanal to the high-tech, and in combinations of the two. Through the creative work, there is a reflection on the relationship between designer and technology as well as on the relationship between technological tools and visual expression. The exploration and understanding of the connection between traditional craftsmanship and new technology is central. The transformation of our lives and economy in recent times, based on the three digital revolutions (data processing, communication, and manufacturing), will be highlighted in the course.</p>\n","isDescriptionOutdated":false,"order":1,"points":7.5,"year":1,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":"Visuell kommunikasjonsdesign","studyOption":null,"category":"specialization"},{"code":"UTV999-MA-30","name":"Utveksling","description":null,"isDescriptionOutdated":null,"order":1,"points":30,"year":2,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":{"label":"Exchange","isDefault":false},"category":"obligatory"},{"code":"VAL999MA15","name":"Valgemne","description":"\r\n<p lang=\"nb-NO\" style=\"text-indent:0pt;margin-left:0pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;margin-right:0pt;font-size:11pt;\">Du kan lese mer om dine valgmuligheter i dette semesteret</p>\r\n<p lang=\"nb-NO\" style=\"text-indent:0pt;margin-left:0pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;margin-right:0pt;font-size:11pt;color:#0000FF;text-decoration:underline ;\"><a href=\"https://kristiania.no/for-studenter/valgemner-og-praksis-4/\" style=\"font-size:11pt;color:#0000FF;text-decoration:underline ;\">https://kristiania.no/for-studenter/valgemner-og-praksis-4/</a></p>\r\n","isDescriptionOutdated":true,"order":1,"points":15,"year":2,"semester":"first","isPrerequisite":false,"specialization":null,"studyOption":{"label":"Default path","isDefault":true},"category":"obligatory"}],"translations":{"studyOptions":"Options","elective":"Elective","specialization":"Specialization","selectYear":"Select year","openSubjectOverlay":"Open subject overlay","closeOverlay":"Close overlay","genericErrorMessage":"Error occured","totalStudyPointsFormat":"{points} total credits","studyPointsFormat":"{points} credits","pointsUnit":"credits","noSubjectsMessage":"Sorry, the content could not be loaded at the moment","prerequisites":"Prerequisites","goToSubject":"Read more","loading":"Loading data","year":"year","semester":"semester","single":"single","first":"first","second":"second","third":"third","fourth":"fourth","fifth":"fifth"},"type":"view","id":"study-model","teaser":"This master's degree lasts two academic years, and each academic year is divided into two semesters. Here you can see an overview of compulsory subjects and what opportunities you have for practice, exchange and specialization. We reserve the right to make changes.","settings":{"disableStudyModel":false,"disabledStudyModelHeading":"Study model information","disabledStudyModelMessage":{"value":"The study model is being worked on and will be published as soon as it is ready"}},"studyProgramLink":{"label":"See program description","href":"/en/syllabus/faculty-of-society-arts-and-media/second-cycle-degree/mde/master-in-design/","external":false,"showIsExternal":true}};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="b7207905"><section id="career-opportunities" class="cr4GKFNCqp2Yu1c2kbOA"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF vw2qEzBLsG8dfPFKhCCL">Career opportunities</h2><div class="eCTirCs_JIAXtZrCECpB"><div class="s4PUgF87xJpwYCH4qegZ"><div class="gOFfCOGgsoYfQDsrulwN Um_bsxrmr048dYBfonAO hfMRvjcJ6bvRCr56YbNA"><div class="KgSPjnWKLeiPJ0FM0dQy RichtextContent RichtextContent--dark">With this Master's degree in Design, you are qualified for a range of exciting jobs in both the private and public sectors. Many designers specializing in interior architecture and visual communication design currently work as:</p>
<ul class="ml-4 list-outside">
<li>Employees in design agencies</li>
<li>In-house designers in larger companies</li>
<li>Designers and design consultants in consulting firms</li>
<li>Employees at interior architecture offices and architecture firms</li>
<li>Self-employed</li>
</ul>
<p>With a master's degree, you can also qualify for admission to further PhD studies.</div><div></div></div></div></div></div></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['b7207905-json'] = {"id":"career-opportunities","heading":{"value":"Career opportunities"},"teaser":{"value":"With this Master's degree in Design, you are qualified for a range of exciting jobs in both the private and public sectors. Many designers specializing in interior architecture and visual communication design currently work as:</p>\n<ul class=\"ml-4 list-outside\">\n<li>Employees in design agencies</li>\n<li>In-house designers in larger companies</li>\n<li>Designers and design consultants in consulting firms</li>\n<li>Employees at interior architecture offices and architecture firms</li>\n<li>Self-employed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With a master's degree, you can also qualify for admission to further PhD studies."},"linkList":[],"featuredArticle":null,"blockVariant":"default","overlayButtonLabel":null,"overlayHeading":null,"overlayContent":null,"overlayCloseLabel":"Close overlay"};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="45f28bc3"><section id="meet-the-faculty" class="xfIZ8IPRSKGxzL5KIyLz ZG9x_vn8GvsrSWPis7bl"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF Bje8lNYEARjKXkaadcjl">Meet the faculty</h2><div class="IP4WLEZAtSgblGP4Felg"><div class="KCVvuotjBI0iy0ATTKfK"><div class="slJnKPGAAIKofsQwQBjL vPKYJ9xnnqorRitmkm3i ssmQS4h9jzEH4V0L4NSN"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, (min-width: 768px) 552px" srcSet="/contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg?width=375 375w, /contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg?width=552 552w, /contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg?width=768 768w, /contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg?width=1536 1536w"/><img src="/contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg?width=552" alt="Studenter ser på grafiske arbeider." style="object-position:74% 46%" loading="lazy"/></picture></div></div><ul class="tZx0pZhK8q3JNoQI8LCj"><li class="i8yQuD9MeLJCD5eDKft2"><div class="cVALO6yJDjmV1Xvkvynu"><div class="WWUAFGePNJBA0_seRWlX"><h3 class="TCpxq20heJlGBaSbNI4y">Our staff</h3><div class="AXbF9ehRSXzzERBwn1XK RichtextContent RichtextContent--light"><span>Associate Professor and Program Director, <strong><a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/linda-eirin-fosse-lien/">Linda Lien</a></strong>. As an interior architect, she combines industry experience with an academic profile. Her research focuses on the concept of home and residential quality of living, explored both individually and through interdisciplinary collaboration.</span></p>
<p><span>Professor <a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/synne-skjulstad/"><strong>Synne Skjulstad</strong></a>, ph.d., in media studies, has extensive experience in studying the intersections between communication and design. She has published internationally on topics such as interface aesthetics, digital media platforms, fashion media, and critical fashion.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span>Associate Professor <a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/fredrik-eive-refsli/"><strong>Fredrik Eive Refsli </strong></a>is a designer-researcher-teacher with two decades of experience spanning graphic design practice and design education. His work focuses on critical co-creation and design&rsquo;s role in systemic change.</span></p>
<p><span>Professor <a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/margaret-rynning/"><strong>Margaret Rynning</strong></a> is a graphic designer and researcher working with speculative and critical design, design activism, and designers&rsquo; social responsibility. A recurring theme in her research is the relationship between humans and nature.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span>Professor <strong><a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/vesma-kontere-mcquillan/">V&eacute;sma Kontere McQuillan</a></strong> is an architect and writer whose research focuses on fashion spaces as a theoretical framework through which fashion is understood in spatial, curatorial, and architectural terms, as outlined in her book Fashion Spaces: A Theoretical View (2020).</span></p>
<p><span>Associate Professor <a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/jarle-fotland/"><strong>Jarle M. Fotland</strong></a>&nbsp;is a furniture designer and interior architect. In both his teaching and his own artistic research, he focuses on the experience of spatial architecture, and concepts such as belonging, displacement and the "spirit of place&raquo;.</span></p>
<p><span>Associate professor <a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/trond-klevgaard/"><strong>Trond Klevgaard</strong></a> is an internationally experienced graphic designer whose interests include typography and generative visual systems. He is also a design historian with a PhD from the Royal College of Art, who focuses on Scandinavian and graphic design.</span></div></div></div></li></ul></div></div></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['45f28bc3-json'] = {"id":"meet-the-faculty","heading":{"value":"Meet the faculty"},"testimonies":[{"heading":{"value":"Our staff"},"content":{"value":"<span>Associate Professor and Program Director, <strong><a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/linda-eirin-fosse-lien/\">Linda Lien</a></strong>. As an interior architect, she combines industry experience with an academic profile. Her research focuses on the concept of home and residential quality of living, explored both individually and through interdisciplinary collaboration.</span></p>\n<p><span>Professor <a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/synne-skjulstad/\"><strong>Synne Skjulstad</strong></a>, ph.d., in media studies, has extensive experience in studying the intersections between communication and design. She has published internationally on topics such as interface aesthetics, digital media platforms, fashion media, and critical fashion.&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p><span>Associate Professor <a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/fredrik-eive-refsli/\"><strong>Fredrik Eive Refsli </strong></a>is a designer-researcher-teacher with two decades of experience spanning graphic design practice and design education. His work focuses on critical co-creation and design&rsquo;s role in systemic change.</span></p>\n<p><span>Professor <a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/margaret-rynning/\"><strong>Margaret Rynning</strong></a> is a graphic designer and researcher working with speculative and critical design, design activism, and designers&rsquo; social responsibility. A recurring theme in her research is the relationship between humans and nature.&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p><span>Professor <strong><a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/vesma-kontere-mcquillan/\">V&eacute;sma Kontere McQuillan</a></strong> is an architect and writer whose research focuses on fashion spaces as a theoretical framework through which fashion is understood in spatial, curatorial, and architectural terms, as outlined in her book Fashion Spaces: A Theoretical View (2020).</span></p>\n<p><span>Associate Professor <a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/jarle-fotland/\"><strong>Jarle M. Fotland</strong></a>&nbsp;is a furniture designer and interior architect. In both his teaching and his own artistic research, he focuses on the experience of spatial architecture, and concepts such as belonging, displacement and the \"spirit of place&raquo;.</span></p>\n<p><span>Associate professor <a href=\"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/trond-klevgaard/\"><strong>Trond Klevgaard</strong></a> is an internationally experienced graphic designer whose interests include typography and generative visual systems. He is also a design historian with a PhD from the Royal College of Art, who focuses on Scandinavian and graphic design.</span>"}}],"gobiStoryId":null,"imageFallback":{"author":null,"caption":null,"url":"/contentassets/966c03f183564e4a9a11b148a2775a50/due_1679.jpg","alt":"Studenter ser på grafiske arbeider.","focalPoint":{"x":0.74,"y":0.46},"lazyLoading":true},"translations":{"openPlayer":"Open player","failedToLoadVideo":"Sorry, for some reason we could not load a video"},"variant":"dark"};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="9f747e0b"><section id="meet-the-study-program-leader" class="cPloAW9ETJppiKf3uOg6 PS9OiuwCp_Mtf4jcUm_I"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><div class="OPDkk3SbGwWTZeDlZj4f"><div class="Za0F_aizRoRV7IW8vrhK"><span class="SvgIcon R8xM2ukm6YYDNzHE9e8V"><svg><use xlink:href="/dist/iconsSprite.ff403ecca.svg#sprite-quote-mark"></use></svg></span></div><div class="OBg8GbGOQ11Y_kO0y26Q"><span>I have over 20 years of experience as an interior architect and designer, both in agencies and as an independent entrepreneur, and have undertaken many projects for both the public and private sectors.</span></div><div class="WyTuSgXamI5tDjjbW40k"><div class="ELZbSN0xkGRxudxoCGZD Jjpdm0z1lffwiYq7tJFx"><div class="slJnKPGAAIKofsQwQBjL BsNJMoWrLSwywFoZw_rI"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 479px) 100vw, (min-width: 480px) 350px" srcSet="/contentassets/dcc8aef6d7614616bc29995b2d196cb1/linda_lien.jpg?width=350 350w, /contentassets/dcc8aef6d7614616bc29995b2d196cb1/linda_lien.jpg?width=470 470w"/><img src="/contentassets/dcc8aef6d7614616bc29995b2d196cb1/linda_lien.jpg?width=258" alt="Linda Eirin Fosse Lien" loading="lazy"/></picture></div><div class="wgsfEYhliRuQ3OGsPPB7"><a href="/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/linda-eirin-fosse-lien/" class="XezMfDvRba8HKhHr79wm B5Z4_vbRcPt248iwmQXi A9IrxP6x1kHMxJBX5nkl" data-owned=""><div class="DdPYOTimBd1c8zDeoFgi"><span class="PafA02TiaN5DOnBONInU">Linda Eirin Fosse Lien</span><span class="C7aWfJ5wcSGxkMQk5mVC"><ul class="ybs02MiIsdu5yS7LYwML"><li class="MkfboJs3JDCOgTmDA6XY">Associate Professor</li></ul><span class="WNOGE5Zjbw73YZqwUfmg">Department of Creativity and Design</span></span></div></a></div></div></div></div></div></div></section><script>(window.__reactProps = window.__reactProps || {})['9f747e0b-json'] = {"id":"meet-the-study-program-leader","quote":{"value":"<span>I have over 20 years of experience as an interior architect and designer, both in agencies and as an independent entrepreneur, and have undertaken many projects for both the public and private sectors.</span>"},"employeeInfo":{"name":"Linda Eirin Fosse Lien","picture":{"profile":{"defaultWidth":258,"format":"preserve","maxImageDimension":null,"sources":[{"mediaCondition":null,"allowedWidths":[350,470],"sizes":["(max-width: 479px) 100vw","(min-width: 480px) 350px"],"mode":"default","targetAspectRatio":{"ratio":-1,"hasValue":false},"quality":{"quality":-1}}]},"author":null,"caption":null,"url":"/contentassets/dcc8aef6d7614616bc29995b2d196cb1/linda_lien.jpg","alt":"Linda Eirin Fosse Lien","focalPoint":null,"lazyLoading":true},"jobTitles":["Associate Professor"],"department":"Department of Creativity and Design","organization":"Kristiania University of Applied Sciences","link":{"label":"Linda Eirin Fosse Lien","href":"/en/about-kristiania/employees/fakultet-for-samfunn-kunst-og-medier/department-of-creativity-and-design/linda-eirin-fosse-lien/","external":false,"showIsExternal":true}},"variant":"red"};</script></div>
</div><div>
<div id="6d534607"><section id="campus-life" class="pFTWXiiPpytGWLiKLpv9"><div class="Fov3KMlE7osNR74JI44t"><div class="Wf6h4ZdN2S9XvO6RoMCf"><div class="S2f6deV1yP8g5eUszWRn"><h2 class="FrR9v_ssranAjR5kDzwF UGfnqOV55kXJ4kMKpeEI">Campus life</h2><div class="iadlztdFwcV0nqnyj_1B"><div class="ZIEayLuChE50GXCcqJ3_ RichtextContent RichtextContent--light"><span>We dare to claim that Kristiania has one of the best student environments in the country. We actively work to ensure that everyone feels they fit in, regardless of their background, and offer a wide range of activities, events, student associations, and social meeting places. In addition, we ensure small class sizes, close follow-up, and interdisciplinary projects, which help to strengthen the sense of community and make everyone feel included.</span></div></div></div><ul class="YEXvUjfm6kfiJ5E0RUmA"><li class="BJhDL1ivRTBTTz1M1jNJ"><div class="cebw0X3OhBm1h7iQsiYf EsE9RThZNAFgiUOaDSqu vXp_Fkav2sqPTRRNnREp HuwFiB0Vkd6mPM9EtyRn"><div class="slJnKPGAAIKofsQwQBjL vPKYJ9xnnqorRitmkm3i"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 767px) 90vw, (min-width: 768px) 45vw" srcSet="/contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=260 260w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=340 340w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=410 410w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=560 560w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=675 675w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=710 710w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=810 810w, /contentassets/17cdb0eac7654c549d50f5ae2361291f/utstilling_ma_design_akedemisk_poster-12.jpg?width=870 87